is exactly candidate 2 with each pair of days combined into one order (for example, 26+25 = 52 and 54+40 = 94).\n\n- **Candidate 1 vs 2:**\n  - Ordering on 13 days instead of 26 saves 13 × 28 = 364 in fees.\n  - The second day's half of each order sits in stock one extra night: 414 × 0.4 + 535 × 0.5 ≈ 433. On those two items alone candidate 1 is about 69 behind.\n  - But candidate 1 has the next day's stock on hand on the first day of each cycle. That avoids shortages candidate 2 can't, worth about 2 units of product 0 and 4 of product 1 on day 4 alone, and fewer later as a buffer builds.\n  - I estimate about 9 units of each product saved, worth 13–19 each (lost sale + penalty), so roughly +290.\n  - Net, candidate 1 is about 150–220 ahead of candidate 2.\n- **Candidate 3 vs 2:** Candidate 3 cuts the last four orders (arriving days 26–29) by 11 units of each product. By then candidate 2 has most likely built up a surplus, so the cut saves about 11 × 5 + 11 × 9 ≈ 154 in purchases, minus a small extra shortage risk. That puts it about 125–150 ahead of candidate 2.\n\n**Risk.** Candidates 1, 2 and 3 handle swings in demand about equally well. Candidate 1 is slightly more robust early on, because each delivery covers two days."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 361, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5711, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 361}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-26T09:38:35.668Z", "request_id": "req_011CgQd36SrhGt3rm7sk5gzU"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "conte